# bkit on Cursor — mandatory default

**The user does NOT need to say "use bkit".** For every coding task in this repo, you MUST proactively run the bkit workflow unless the user explicitly opts out (e.g. "bkit 없이", "quick fix only", "skip docs").

## Default behavior (every session, every task)

1. **Before coding** — Infer feature name; check `docs/` and MCP `bkit-pdca` for existing PDCA state. If none, start at plan or read `bkit/skills/pdca/SKILL.md` for the right phase.
2. **While coding** — Align with `docs/02-design/` if a design exists; apply `bkit/skills/phase-*` or `development-pipeline` when the task matches a phase.
3. **After meaningful code changes** — Proactively run gap-style verification: read `bkit/skills/pdca/SKILL.md` analyze/iterate sections or spawn **Task** with `bkit/agents/gap-detector.md`. Do not skip quality gates (M1/M3/S1).
4. **Non-trivial features** — Prefer parallel **Task** subagents (2–4 at once) using `bkit/agents/*.md` (e.g. security + frontend + QA) without waiting for the user to ask for "team mode".
5. **Multi-feature or release work** — Use `bkit/skills/sprint/SKILL.md` (master-plan / start) without being asked.
6. **Document** — Write or update Korean docs under `docs/01-plan`, `02-design`, `03-analysis`, `04-report` as phases complete.

If unsure which skill applies, read `bkit/skills/bkit/SKILL.md` first, then the specific skill.

This project includes **bkit** (vendored under `bkit/`). It is the **only** approved development process for this repo—not an optional add-on.

## Language

- Reply to the user in **Korean**.
- Code, commits, PR text, and non-`docs/` markdown stay in **English** (see `bkit/.claude/CLAUDE.md`).
- Planning artifacts under `docs/01-plan/`, `docs/02-design/`, `docs/03-analysis/`, `docs/04-report/` are written in **Korean** unless the user asks otherwise.

## Command mapping (Claude Code → Cursor)

| User intent | Action |
|-------------|--------|
| `/pdca`, `/sprint`, `/control`, `bkit help` | Read `bkit/skills/bkit/SKILL.md`, then the skill for that workflow (`pdca`, `sprint`, `control`) |
| PDCA phase (plan, design, do, analyze, iterate, qa, report) | Read `bkit/skills/pdca/SKILL.md` and follow the phase; use `bkit/agents/*.md` personas via **Task** subagents when the skill names an agent |
| Sprint (master-plan, start, status, …) | Read `bkit/skills/sprint/SKILL.md` |
| Quality / gap / review | `gap-detector`, `code-analyzer`, `design-validator` → Task with matching `bkit/agents/<name>.md` |
| PM / team / QA teams | `pm-lead`, `qa-lead`, `cto-lead` agents under `bkit/agents/` |
| Pipeline phases 1–9 | `bkit/skills/phase-*` and `development-pipeline` |

Slash commands are **documentation labels**. In Cursor, trigger the same behavior by naming the action in natural language (Korean or English) or by saying e.g. "pdca plan user-auth".

## MCP tools

When PDCA status, doc paths, or gap metrics are needed, use MCP servers **bkit-pdca** and **bkit-analysis** (configured in `.cursor/mcp.json`). Set `BKIT_ROOT` to the workspace root.

## State and config

- Config: `bkit.config.json` (copy or symlink from `bkit/bkit.config.json` at repo root if missing).
- Runtime state: `.bkit/` (gitignored) — create on first PDCA/sprint run.
- Doc layout: `docs/01-plan/`, `docs/02-design/`, `docs/03-analysis/`, `docs/04-report/` per `bkit.config.json` `pdca.docPaths`.

## Quality gates (summary)

Do not advance PDCA/sprint phases when gates fail: **M1** match rate &lt; 90% → iterate (max 5); **M3** critical issues → stop; **S1** dataFlow &lt; 85% → re-verify. Details in `bkit/README.md` and `bkit/skills/pdca/SKILL.md`.

## Skills discovery

Project skills are linked under `.cursor/skills/` (run `scripts/setup-bkit-cursor.sh` after clone). You may also read skills directly from `bkit/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`.

## Subagents

For parallel specialist work, use the **Task** tool with prompts derived from `bkit/agents/<agent>.md` frontmatter and body. See root `AGENTS.md` for a quick index.

## IoT project

Application code for this repo lives outside `bkit/`; use bkit for **how** you plan, design, verify, and document features—not as runtime dependency unless explicitly integrated.
